---
import cli from '../../../../packages/cli/package.json'
import DemoStatus from '../components/DemoStatus.astro'
import Next from '../components/Next.astro'
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro'
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal'
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
---

<Page
  page="test"
  title="Test"
  description="The same request or flow, run by QA with a click and by CI on every change, with the same checks and the same readable failures."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">04 · Test</span>
      <h1 class="display">The same file. QA clicks it, CI runs it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A request says what a pass is. A flow says that something a user does works: "can book an order".
        QA runs them from a published portal; CI runs them on every change, with the same checks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" style="padding-top: 8px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="title">Assertions say what worked</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A request with no <code>assert</code> passes on a 2xx response. Write one and it is judged by that
          alone. The helpers are
          <a href="https://github.com/lukeed/uvu/blob/master/docs/api.assert.md">uvu/assert</a>; every check runs
          and is reported, passing or failing, with what was expected and what came back.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/requests/petstore/find-pets.req.ts" region="request" run="try:petstore/FindPetsByStatus" />

      <h2 class="title">Flows have inputs</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A flow has <strong>inputs</strong>, like a GitHub workflow's inputs or Storybook's controls. In the
          portal they are controls you change before you press Run; on the command line they are
          <code>--input name=value</code>. Inputs carry defaults, so a flow runs unattended in CI.
        </p>
        <p>
          An input can start from generated data. QA gets a realistic name without typing one, can press
          Generate for another, or press <strong>Run with defaults</strong> to skip every question.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/sequences/new-pet.seq.ts" region="flow" run="flow:new-pet" />
      <p><DemoStatus /></p>

      <h2 class="title">QA: a portal that is only what you test</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>specbook build --minimal</code> publishes the operations your requests and flows use, and leaves
          the rest of the API out. QA opens it, picks a request or a flow, chooses an environment and presses Run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><a class="btn primary" href="/qa/">Open the QA portal ↗</a></p>

      <h2 class="title">CI: the same checks on every change</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This is the runner CI uses, on the page. Choose what the demo API does and press Run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Terminal client:visible files={['find-pets', 'adopt-pet']} chaos={['calm', 'down', 'wrong', 'flaky']} version={cli.version} />
      <Snippet file="snippets/test-workflow.yml" region="workflow" title="test.yml" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Secrets are environment variables named in the config, so a CI secret store is all it needs. Under
          GitHub Actions the failures are annotated on the change, with a summary on the run. The exit code is 0
          (passed), 1 (a flow failed or errored), 2 (it could not run) or 3 (usage).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><Next page="test" /></div>
</Page>
